---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base title="About" description="How the Atlas of Justice works, where the drawings come from and how to add a case.">
  <section class="about">
    <h1>About the Atlas</h1>
    <p>
      The Atlas collects trials and hearings from many countries. Each case has a place on the map, a short
      description, the outcome and a list of sources. Cameras are often barred from courtrooms, so for many
      famous cases the public knew the proceedings through the drawings of court artists.
    </p>

    <h2>The drawings</h2>
    <p>
      Only cases that have a courtroom drawing are on the map. The drawings are not copied to this site: each one is
      loaded directly from the archive that hosts it (Wikimedia Commons, the Library of Congress, a university
      collection) and is shown with its artist, a credit line and a link back to the original record. Rights differ
      between archives and between works: nineteenth-century engravings are in the public domain, while some
      twentieth-century drawings are still protected, and the credit line says what the host archive states. If you
      hold rights to a drawing and want it removed, open an issue in the repository.
    </p>

    <h2>The map</h2>
    <p>
      A pin marks the city or district of the court, not a street address. Base map: OpenStreetMap contributors.
    </p>

    <h2>Adding a case</h2>
    <p>
      Each case is one Markdown file in <code>src/content/cases/</code>: data at the top, the write-up below. A case is
      shown only if it has an <code>image</code> block with the URL of the drawing; set <code>theme</code> to give it its own look.
    </p>
    <pre><code>---
title: Name of the proceeding
country: Country
city: City
court: Name of the court
region: europe        # europe, north-america, south-america, africa, asia, oceania
category: political   # international, transitional, political, miscarriage, society, hearing, crime
lat: 48.85
lng: 2.35
yearStart: 1899
yearEnd: 1906         # optional
summary: One or two sentences for the card.
outcome: How it ended.
theme: my-case       # optional, matches a [data-theme] block in global.css
image:
  src: https://example.org/drawing.jpg
  alt: What the drawing shows
  artist: Artist name
  license: Credit / rights note
  sourceUrl: https://example.org/record
sources:
  - title: Source title
    url: https://example.org/source
---

Two or three paragraphs of description.</code></pre>
  </section>
</Base>
